C = [
("h1", "5. Jeremiah: The Temple Sermon and the Hardest Sentence"),
("p", "Claim: Jeremiah’s sermon at the gate of the LORD’s house is the fullest statement of what the prophets say about worship by those who trust the house, and it holds a sentence that seems to deny that the LORD ever commanded sacrifices. Read with the verses around it and with Exodus 19, that sentence is a statement of rank: the first word at the Exodus was obedience. Jeremiah himself speaks of burnt offerings, sacrifices of praise and sacrifices continually in the house of the LORD in the days of restoration. His judgment falls on a house trusted as a charm, and the pattern of Shiloh is the sentence."),
("sub", "5.1 Incense from Sheba"),
("p", "The first sentence is in chapter 6. “Hear, O earth: behold, I will bring evil upon this people, even the fruit of their thoughts, because they have not hearkened unto my words, nor to my law, but rejected it” (Jeremiah 6:19). The charge is first of all that the people have not hearkened and have rejected the law. Then comes the question about the offerings: “To what purpose cometh there to me incense from Sheba, and the sweet cane from a far country? your burnt offerings are not acceptable, nor your sacrifices sweet unto me” (Jeremiah 6:20)."),
("p", "The incense came from far away and was costly, and the sweet cane was a rare spice. The people spared no expense for the house. The LORD says the burnt offerings are not acceptable and the sacrifices are not sweet to him. The offerings are the people’s: “your burnt offerings,” “your sacrifices.” The reason is in verse 19, which comes before the verse and gives the ground of it: the law rejected. The next verse says what follows, “I will lay stumblingblocks before this people” (Jeremiah 6:21). The sentence on the offerings is a sentence on those who reject the law in the same house in which they bring incense."),
("sub", "5.2 The sermon at the gate"),
("p", "Chapter 7 gives the setting of the sermon in the opening verses. “Stand in the gate of the LORD’s house, and proclaim there this word, and say, Hear the word of the LORD, all ye of Judah, that enter in at these gates to worship the LORD” (Jeremiah 7:2). The audience is the worshipers entering to worship. The message is conditional: “Amend your ways and your doings, and I will cause you to dwell in this place” (Jeremiah 7:3)."),
("p", "The next verse names the false confidence. “Trust ye not in lying words, saying, The temple of the LORD, The temple of the LORD, The temple of the LORD, are these” (Jeremiah 7:4). The repetition is the prophet’s way of quoting a chant. The people say the name of the house three times as if the name protected them. The prophet calls it “lying words, that cannot profit” (Jeremiah 7:8). The condition is repeated: “if ye throughly amend your ways and your doings; if ye throughly execute judgment between a man and his neighbour; if ye oppress not the stranger, the fatherless, and the widow, and shed not innocent blood in this place, neither walk after other gods to your hurt: then will I cause you to dwell in this place, in the land that I gave to your fathers, for ever and ever” (Jeremiah 7:5–7)."),
("p", "The LORD does not say the house is wrong, and he does not say the people should stay away from it. He says that dwelling in the place depends on how they live in it. The “for ever and ever” belongs to the conditional word of Jeremiah 18:7–10, read in Volume 1, and it is not a sworn word."),
("sub", "5.3 A den of robbers"),
("p", "Then the charge: “Will ye steal, murder, and commit adultery, and swear falsely, and burn incense unto Baal, and walk after other gods whom ye know not; and come and stand before me in this house, which is called by my name, and say, We are delivered to do all these abominations?” (Jeremiah 7:9, 10). The picture is of a worshiper who sins and then comes to stand in the house, and who says that the standing there delivers him to go on. The house is “called by my name” twice in two verses. The LORD claims it. Then the question: “Is this house, which is called by my name, become a den of robbers in your eyes? Behold, even I have seen it, saith the LORD” (Jeremiah 7:11). A den is where robbers go to hide between robberies. The house has become the place where the people hide from the consequences of what they do outside it."),
("sub", "5.4 Shiloh"),
("p", "The sentence is in the pattern of a precedent. “But go ye now unto my place which was in Shiloh, where I set my name at the first, and see what I did to it for the wickedness of my people Israel” (Jeremiah 7:12). Shiloh was the place where the LORD had set his name first. The ark was there in the days of Eli and Samuel. The LORD says it was destroyed for the wickedness of his people. Therefore, “will I do unto this house, which is called by my name, wherein ye trust, and unto the place which I gave to you and to your fathers, as I have done to Shiloh. And I will cast you out of my sight, as I have cast out all your brethren, even the whole seed of Ephraim” (Jeremiah 7:14, 15)."),
("p", "The precedent shows what the sentence on a house is. A house in which the LORD’s name was set at the first can be abandoned for the wickedness of the people who trust it. The sentence is not that houses of worship are wrong. It is that the house cannot protect those who use it as a shelter. Jeremiah 26 gives the same sermon in a narrative form: “Then will I make this house like Shiloh, and will make this city a curse to all the nations of the earth” (Jeremiah 26:6). The priests and prophets and all the people take Jeremiah and say, “Thou shalt surely die” (Jeremiah 26:8). Volume 1 reads the end of that chapter, where the elders recall Micah and the princes say that Jeremiah is not worthy to die (Jeremiah 26:16)."),
("sub", "5.5 Put your burnt offerings unto your sacrifices, and eat flesh"),
("p", "The next section is the one that has been read most often as a denial of the sacrifices. It begins with a command that is plainly ironic. “Thus saith the LORD of hosts, the God of Israel; Put your burnt offerings unto your sacrifices, and eat flesh” (Jeremiah 7:21). A burnt offering is wholly burned and not eaten. A sacrifice, a peace offering, is partly eaten by the offerer. The LORD says to put the burnt offerings with the sacrifices and eat all the flesh. The command takes away the line between what is the LORD’s and what is the offerer’s. He tells the people that if their offerings are no more than meat, they may eat it. The sentence is the same as Isaiah’s: nothing the LORD sees in them is a gift to him."),
("p", "Then verses 22 and 23. “For I spake not unto your fathers, nor commanded them in the day that I brought them out of the land of Egypt, concerning burnt offerings or sacrifices: but this thing commanded I them, saying, Obey my voice, and I will be your God, and ye shall be my people: and walk ye in all the ways that I have commanded you, that it may be well unto you” (Jeremiah 7:22, 23)."),
("p", "Three observations. First, the sentence says what the LORD commanded the fathers: “Obey my voice, and I will be your God, and ye shall be my people.” That is the covenant formula, and it is found at the first meeting at Sinai: “Now therefore, if ye will obey my voice indeed, and keep my covenant, then ye shall be a peculiar treasure unto me above all people” (Exodus 19:5). The words “obey my voice” are the opening condition of the covenant. The sacrifices, the tabernacle and the feasts come after them. Jeremiah 7:22 says that in the day of the Exodus and in the first words the LORD spoke to the fathers, the word was obedience. The offerings came as the ways of the obedient people. Jeremiah is saying what the LORD said first."),
("p", "Second, a flat reading is impossible. The prophet’s own book speaks of burnt offerings, sacrifices and meat offerings in the city of the LORD’s promise. The first passage is conditional on the city’s hearkening and on its keeping the sabbath (Jeremiah 17:24), and it reads: “And they shall come from the cities of Judah … bringing burnt offerings, and sacrifices, and meat offerings, and incense, and bringing sacrifices of praise, unto the house of the LORD” (Jeremiah 17:26). In another chapter the LORD promises that the voice of joy shall be heard again, “and of them that shall bring the sacrifice of praise into the house of the LORD” (Jeremiah 33:11), and that “the priests the Levites” shall not want a man “to offer burnt offerings, and to kindle meat offerings, and to do sacrifice continually” (Jeremiah 33:18). The same prophet cannot mean that the LORD never wanted burnt offerings and sacrifices. The volume reports these verses as written and does not argue the covenants they sit beside, which are read in the Covenantal and Prophetic Identity series."),
("p", "Third, the idiom is the same as in Hosea and Psalm 40. A not-but sentence of rank can say not this but that when it means this above all, and not this alone. The comparison here is with the fathers at the Exodus: the first word to them was not a code of sacrifice but a call to obey. The phrase “in the day that I brought them out” covers the season of the Exodus. The Passover kept in that season was a sacrifice (Exodus 12), and so the sentence, taken as a flat denial, would contradict the story it appeals to. It is the rank of the first word that the prophet states."),
("p", "A contrast in the same chapter fixes the usage. At Tophet in the valley of Hinnom the people “burn their sons and their daughters in the fire; which I commanded them not, neither came it into my heart” (Jeremiah 7:31). Here the LORD says he did not command a thing, and means that it is foreign to him. If verse 22 said the same of burnt offerings, it would put the sacrifices of Leviticus on a level with the burning of children, and no one reads Jeremiah that way. The two sentences are different. In verse 22 the LORD says the first word was obedience, and in verse 31 he says the thing never came into his heart."),
("ctbl", "Jeremiah 6–7 in six passages.", [
    ("Passage", "What the LORD says", "What it shows"),
    ("Jeremiah 6:19, 20", "The law is rejected; the burnt offerings are not acceptable nor the sacrifices sweet", "The offerings are “your” offerings; the ground is the law rejected"),
    ("Jeremiah 7:2–7", "Amend your ways, and I will cause you to dwell in this place", "A conditional word at the gate of the house"),
    ("Jeremiah 7:4, 8–11", "“The temple of the LORD” three times is lying words; the house is a den of robbers in their eyes", "The house is trusted as a shelter by those who sin outside it"),
    ("Jeremiah 7:12–15", "Go to Shiloh and see what I did to it", "The sentence on a house is judgment on those who use it; a precedent, not an abolition"),
    ("Jeremiah 7:21–23", "Put your burnt offerings unto your sacrifices; I spake not concerning them, but said Obey my voice", "The first word at the Exodus was obedience (Exodus 19:5); a statement of rank"),
    ("Jeremiah 7:31", "The burning of children at Tophet I commanded not, neither came it into my heart", "The LORD says elsewhere that he did not command a thing and means that it is foreign to him"),
  ], [0.2, 0.44, 0.36]),
("sub", "5.6 What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "Jeremiah 6:19 gives the ground of the verdict on the offerings: the people have rejected the law.",
    "The sermon at the gate is a conditional word, addressed to those who enter the house to worship, and it names the house as the LORD’s: “called by my name.”",
    "The sentence on the house is the precedent of Shiloh, a judgment on a house trusted as a shelter by those who steal, murder and swear falsely.",
    "Jeremiah 7:22, 23 states what the LORD said first, “Obey my voice” (Exodus 19:5); it is read as a statement of rank and not as a denial that the offerings were commanded.",
    "Jeremiah 17:26 (the city that hearkens) and 33:11, 18 (the return of the captivity) speak of burnt offerings, sacrifices of praise and sacrifice continually in the house of the LORD.",
  ]),
]
